{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>This is the Torah.</b> That is, this which we said at the beginning of the pericah is the statute of the Torah." "<b>Every one who comes into the tent.</b> Lest you say: regarding touching the dead the verse was stringent, but regarding entering the tent it was not stringent — it says, 'every one who comes into the tent.' <b>And all that is in the tent</b> — why is this said? If 'who comes into the tent' implies that he entered part of it, he is impure, but 'in the tent,' which implies he entered all of it — is it not an a fortiori inference that he should be impure? But since one does not punish by inference, it wrote for you 'and all that is in the tent.' And there are interpreters: <b>every one who comes into the tent</b> implies while the dead body is still in it, even momentarily, even living creatures that are accustomed to enter contract impurity. <b>And all that is in the tent</b> — movable objects contract impurity even if they were there before he died and are still there now."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:18:41.265508179Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}